1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,320
You want to send a friend one answer, yes or no, over a crackly radio.

2
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:11,400
You send a one for yes. But static flips it to a zero, and your friend hears no.

3
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,674
The oldest fix is to say it three times.

4
00:00:14,674 --> 00:00:21,070
You send one, one, one. Static flips one of them, your friend hears one, zero, one, takes

5
00:00:21,070 --> 00:00:24,016
a majority vote, and the answer survives.

6
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:27,869
But that costs three symbols for every one you meant.

7
00:00:27,869 --> 00:00:32,320
So, how little extra can you send and still repair any single error?

8
00:00:33,299 --> 00:00:37,843
Here are all eight messages of three bits, as the corners of a cube.

9
00:00:37,843 --> 00:00:40,649
Flipping one bit moves you along one edge.

10
00:00:40,649 --> 00:00:45,593
The repetition code only uses two corners, zero zero zero and one one one.

11
00:00:45,593 --> 00:00:50,003
They are three edges apart, so we say the code has distance three.

12
00:00:50,399 --> 00:00:55,747
Draw a bubble around each codeword: the codeword plus everything one flip away.

13
00:00:55,747 --> 00:01:01,569
The bubbles do not overlap. So after one error you still know which bubble you are in,

14
00:01:01,569 --> 00:01:02,855
and you can fix it.

15
00:01:03,699 --> 00:01:06,392
There is another way to find the error.

16
00:01:06,392 --> 00:01:09,362
Instead of reading the bits, ask questions.

17
00:01:09,362 --> 00:01:13,160
Do bits one and two agree? Do bits two and three agree?

18
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:15,507
Write zero for yes and one for no.

19
00:01:15,899 --> 00:01:20,396
No error answers zero, zero. An error on bit one gives one, zero.

20
00:01:20,396 --> 00:01:23,855
Bit two gives one, one. Bit three gives zero, one.

21
00:01:23,855 --> 00:01:26,691
Each position leaves its own fingerprint.

22
00:01:26,691 --> 00:01:30,011
And the answers never reveal the message itself.

23
00:01:30,866 --> 00:01:36,140
Now, efficiency. With r yes-or-no questions there are two to the r answer lists.

24
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:41,282
One must mean all clear, and every position needs a different list of its own.

25
00:01:41,666 --> 00:01:46,310
Three questions give eight lists: one for all clear, seven for positions.

26
00:01:46,310 --> 00:01:50,636
So three questions protect seven bits, leaving four for the message.

27
00:01:50,636 --> 00:01:52,226
That is the Hamming code.

28
00:01:52,599 --> 00:01:57,868
Think of each fingerprint as a point. The code works exactly when all the points are

29
00:01:57,868 --> 00:02:00,063
different. Hold on to that picture.

30
00:02:00,899 --> 00:02:04,492
Quantum computers need this too, with two new problems.

31
00:02:04,492 --> 00:02:08,411
First, you may not look: measuring quantum data disturbs it.

32
00:02:08,799 --> 00:02:14,709
But our questions never ask what a value is, only whether values agree, and questions

33
00:02:14,709 --> 00:02:17,350
like that can be asked without damage.

34
00:02:17,350 --> 00:02:21,522
Second, quantum data can be damaged in two independent ways.

35
00:02:21,522 --> 00:02:23,607
Call them a shift and a twist.

36
00:02:24,466 --> 00:02:28,487
We will use qudits: quantum dials with q settings.

37
00:02:28,487 --> 00:02:33,312
A qutrit has q equal to three, so it reads zero, one or two.

38
00:02:33,312 --> 00:02:40,228
Its arithmetic is clock arithmetic: two plus two wraps round to one, and two times two

39
00:02:40,228 --> 00:02:41,434
is one as well.

40
00:02:41,833 --> 00:02:48,379
A shift turns the dial by one or two. A twist, the invisible kind, also comes in amounts

41
00:02:48,379 --> 00:02:53,735
one or two. That is nine combinations, one of which is no damage at all.

42
00:02:53,735 --> 00:02:56,785
So each qutrit has eight possible errors.

43
00:02:57,633 --> 00:03:02,256
Let us ask five questions, each answered on the clock.

44
00:03:02,256 --> 00:03:09,876
These numbers are from a real code. For qutrit fourteen, a shift by one gives the answers

45
00:03:09,876 --> 00:03:16,041
zero, two, one, two, one. A twist by one gives one, zero, one, one, one.

46
00:03:16,433 --> 00:03:22,558
A shift by s with a twist by t gives s times the first list plus t times the second.

47
00:03:22,558 --> 00:03:28,099
That makes nine lists, a flat sheet of answers belonging to this one qutrit.

48
00:03:28,099 --> 00:03:33,641
To repair every error, two qutrits' sheets may share only the all-zero list.

49
00:03:34,499 --> 00:03:40,173
Now count. Five questions give three to the five, two hundred and forty-three, answer

50
00:03:40,173 --> 00:03:43,844
lists. One means all clear, and each qutrit uses eight.

51
00:03:43,844 --> 00:03:49,451
So one plus eight n is at most two hundred and forty-three, and n is at most thirty.

52
00:03:49,833 --> 00:03:52,950
Public tables record the best codes known.

53
00:03:52,950 --> 00:03:57,701
For five questions on qutrits they had a code with twenty-eight.

54
00:03:57,701 --> 00:04:00,745
For twenty-nine, the entry said: unknown.

55
00:04:00,745 --> 00:04:04,605
So is the limit twenty-eight, twenty-nine or thirty?

56
00:04:04,605 --> 00:04:05,793
Pause and guess.

57
00:04:07,833 --> 00:04:13,254
Here is the key translation. Double an answer list on the clock, and ones become twos.

58
00:04:13,254 --> 00:04:16,785
Glue each list to its double, and call the pair a point.

59
00:04:17,166 --> 00:04:22,868
The two hundred and forty-two nonzero lists become one hundred and twenty-one points.

60
00:04:22,868 --> 00:04:27,698
One qutrit's sheet becomes just four points, and those four form a line.

61
00:04:27,698 --> 00:04:31,723
The rule becomes: the lines of different qutrits never meet.

62
00:04:31,723 --> 00:04:33,870
Lines like that are called skew.

63
00:04:36,966 --> 00:04:40,510
You have seen skew lines: a road under a bridge.

64
00:04:40,510 --> 00:04:45,678
Not parallel, yet never touching, because there is room to pass above.

65
00:04:46,866 --> 00:04:53,734
In a plane there is no such room. This is the smallest finite plane: seven points, seven

66
00:04:53,734 --> 00:04:56,466
lines. Any two lines share a point.

67
00:04:56,866 --> 00:05:02,790
With four questions on qutrits there is room: forty points, covered exactly by ten skew

68
00:05:02,790 --> 00:05:08,577
lines. That is a ten-qutrit code, right at the counting limit, and codes like it were

69
00:05:08,577 --> 00:05:08,986
known.

70
00:05:09,833 --> 00:05:14,535
Back to five questions and one hundred and twenty-one points.

71
00:05:14,535 --> 00:05:21,166
Suppose twenty-nine skew lines fit. They cover one hundred and sixteen points, leaving

72
00:05:21,166 --> 00:05:23,633
five uncovered. Call them holes.

73
00:05:24,033 --> 00:05:27,876
Now take a slice: all the points that obey one fixed equation.

74
00:05:27,876 --> 00:05:33,517
Every slice has forty points, and every line either lies inside it or crosses it at exactly

75
00:05:33,517 --> 00:05:34,137
one point.

76
00:05:34,533 --> 00:05:40,812
So a slice has forty minus twenty-nine holes, minus three more for each line lying inside

77
00:05:40,812 --> 00:05:43,563
it. That is eleven, eight, five or two.

78
00:05:43,563 --> 00:05:45,045
Never fewer than two.

79
00:05:45,433 --> 00:05:51,045
One hundred and twenty-one slices, each with at least two holes, need two hundred and

80
00:05:51,045 --> 00:05:56,724
forty-two hole-slice pairs. But each hole lies in exactly forty slices, and five times

81
00:05:56,724 --> 00:05:58,441
forty is only two hundred.

82
00:05:58,833 --> 00:06:01,564
Contradiction. Twenty-nine cannot fit.

83
00:06:01,564 --> 00:06:04,295
The limit is twenty-eight, not thirty.

84
00:06:04,295 --> 00:06:09,039
This hole count for skew lines goes back to Beutelspacher in 1975.

85
00:06:09,039 --> 00:06:12,993
What is new is applying it to every one of these codes.

86
00:06:13,832 --> 00:06:20,125
Including cheaters. Some codes let two errors give the same answers, when their difference

87
00:06:20,125 --> 00:06:25,999
is harmless to the message. We showed this never pays when q is at least three: with

88
00:06:25,999 --> 00:06:31,592
one harmless error, five questions protect at most two plus ten, twelve qutrits.

89
00:06:32,432 --> 00:06:37,127
Can twenty-eight be reached? Think of lines y equals a x.

90
00:06:37,127 --> 00:06:43,880
Different slopes meet only at the centre, the all-zero list, which does not count.

91
00:06:44,266 --> 00:06:49,096
Do the same with five entries. The first two, x and y, are free.

92
00:06:49,096 --> 00:06:55,663
The last three are the slope a times x plus y omega, where a comes from a number system

93
00:06:55,663 --> 00:07:00,418
with twenty-seven elements. That gives twenty-seven skew lines.

94
00:07:00,799 --> 00:07:07,499
None of them touches the leftover plane where x and y are zero, and a plane holds exactly

95
00:07:07,499 --> 00:07:09,607
one more line. Twenty-eight.

96
00:07:10,466 --> 00:07:14,915
One quantum rule remains: the questions must be askable together.

97
00:07:14,915 --> 00:07:20,733
Each line gets a signature of ten clock numbers, and the signatures must add to zero.

98
00:07:20,733 --> 00:07:24,292
Relabelling a qutrit's twists doubles its signature.

99
00:07:24,292 --> 00:07:25,730
Call that its weight.

100
00:07:26,099 --> 00:07:31,743
Watch the running total. The twenty-seven slope lines with weight one add up to zero.

101
00:07:31,743 --> 00:07:34,067
The extra line leaves a single one.

102
00:07:34,466 --> 00:07:37,512
The fix: give three slope lines weight two.

103
00:07:37,512 --> 00:07:43,533
Now they leave a two in that slot, the extra line adds one, and on the clock two plus

104
00:07:43,533 --> 00:07:49,130
one is zero. Twenty-eight qutrits, five questions, every single error repaired.

105
00:07:49,965 --> 00:07:55,895
Here is the full answer, for every dial size q from three up that is a prime or prime

106
00:07:55,895 --> 00:08:01,964
power. A code that repairs any single error exists exactly when there are at least four

107
00:08:01,964 --> 00:08:06,429
questions, and no more qudits than the most skew lines that fit.

108
00:08:06,798 --> 00:08:10,934
For an even number of questions that matches the counting limit.

109
00:08:10,934 --> 00:08:13,648
For an odd number it is q minus one lower.

110
00:08:13,648 --> 00:08:19,141
With five questions it is q cubed plus one: twenty-eight, sixty-five, one hundred and

111
00:08:19,141 --> 00:08:22,566
twenty-six. And every length in between can be built.

112
00:08:23,432 --> 00:08:26,437
That settles entries in the public tables.

113
00:08:26,437 --> 00:08:33,163
There are new codes on four-setting dials with fifty-nine, sixty-one, sixty-two and sixty-five

114
00:08:33,163 --> 00:08:38,744
qudits. Twenty-nine qutrits, and sixty-six four-setting dials, are impossible.

115
00:08:39,132 --> 00:08:44,046
The same argument also settles some codes with larger distance.

116
00:08:44,046 --> 00:08:47,790
Twelve entries improve, and eleven become exact.

117
00:08:47,790 --> 00:08:50,988
Some rows rely on a 1971 theorem of Thas.

118
00:08:51,832 --> 00:08:56,572
How sure is this? The limit and the constructions are short written proofs.

119
00:08:56,572 --> 00:09:02,134
Two hundred and fifty-five explicit codes, the starting cases and the new ones, all pass

120
00:09:02,134 --> 00:09:06,496
an independent checker, which rejects seven deliberately broken ones.

121
00:09:06,865 --> 00:09:10,484
A computer search alone could not rule out twenty-nine.

122
00:09:10,484 --> 00:09:14,761
But the proofs are not checked in Lean and not yet peer reviewed.

123
00:09:14,761 --> 00:09:20,617
They cover stabilizer codes, the standard kind in the tables, and some rows lean on Thas.

124
00:09:21,465 --> 00:09:25,345
So: repairing an error means every error leaves a different answer.

125
00:09:25,345 --> 00:09:30,673
For quantum codes each qudit becomes a line, and the lines must never meet and must balance.

126
00:09:30,673 --> 00:09:34,785
Counting holes gives the limit, and a balanced construction reaches it.

127
00:09:35,165 --> 00:09:40,008
Still unknown: codes with distance four and an odd number of questions.

128
00:09:40,008 --> 00:09:45,874
For qutrits with seven questions, the longest known to us have sixteen qudits, and the

129
00:09:45,874 --> 00:09:48,125
best proven limit is twenty-nine.
